			"author": {
				"name": "Amelia Bellamy-Royds",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/304/885/279/901/783/original/ac1081e7e8ac0f19.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-10-23T21:42:12.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> A lot of the \"perceived performance\" stuff feels like a sleazy way to make sure the user doesn't notice that you're chewing through their data plan &amp; battery lifespan.</p><p>Which might make sense, in an evil genius way, if they're using it to serve more ads or run cryptocurrency frauds in the background. But mostly it just seems to be a way to make things look snappy when you show them to executives in a boardroom to get their sign-off.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
