		{
			"id": "113359181950335156",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://mstdn.social/@peterreeves/113359181919493329",
			"author": {
				"name": "Peter Reeves",
				"url": "https://mstdn.social/@peterreeves",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/571/154/415/137/324/original/1f49a7c45f638268.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-10-23T22:50:45.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> But what if the standard set of measurements aren't capturing actual lived experience? A bog standard MPA might score well in tests, but if every time I change page I have to trek to the server/DB in the US (It's always in the US) and back, then it can be a worse experience than client side routing. And of course CSR isn't really captured in tests, so it's easy for people to point to an MPA and say \"See the official stats? This is always better\".</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113359310598200346",
			"parentId": "113359181950335156",
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff/113359310544726696",
			"author": {
				"name": "jake lazaroff",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/829/533/556/740/279/original/69a92e79689324bc.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-10-23T23:23:27.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mstdn.social/@peterreeves\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>peterreeves</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> i don’t have an opinion on any particular tests but i do think it’s mostly wishful thinking that SPAs that use a client-server architecture (i.e. render a UI on the client with data from a server) will perform better than MPAs on navigation, even in the best case where the JS is cached. in both scenarios navigation is network bound: the user sees nothing meaningful until there’s a round trip from the browser to the DB and back.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113359327416383425",
			"parentId": "113359310598200346",
			"url": "https://mstdn.social/@peterreeves/113359327402601123",
			"author": {
				"name": "Peter Reeves",
				"url": "https://mstdn.social/@peterreeves",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/571/154/415/137/324/original/1f49a7c45f638268.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-10-23T23:27:45.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>jakelazaroff</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> But there are things that SPAs can do to make it faster for me as a human (rather than as a machine test). They can initiate the request when I hover on a link to get a head start, they can reduce the amount of data requested (since they may already have half the app state in memory), they can do optimistic updates, etc.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113359358901536192",
			"parentId": "113359327416383425",
			"url": "https://mstdn.social/@peterreeves/113359358882406417",
			"author": {
				"name": "Peter Reeves",
				"url": "https://mstdn.social/@peterreeves",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/571/154/415/137/324/original/1f49a7c45f638268.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-10-23T23:35:45.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>jakelazaroff</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Additionally, if I'm navigating from page A -&gt; B -&gt; C (assume there is no direct link from A to C, or the user meant to go for C and mis-clicked B), and B is a heavy page (think a table with thousands of items) then an MPA will be slower. Hitting page B in an MPA will require the whole DB + SSR + network dance, before I can just see the link to page C. With CSR, I see a spinner while the items load, but I don't care about them. I can click the link to page C immediately.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113359385089521954",
			"parentId": "113358799087635408",
			"url": "https://anarres.family/@tarajdactyl/113359385044549350",
			"author": {
				"name": "Tara 🕷️:blobbat:",
				"url": "https://anarres.family/@tarajdactyl",
				"avatarUrl": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/avatars/original/missing.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-10-23T23:42:24.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span>  this is why websites are as slow (or slower!) to load now as they were in the 90s, even though broadband Internet is very widespread.</p><p>it is infuriating. i am sure they load and run very quickly on the developer's machines and on the flagship smartphones connected to high quality Wi-Fi with an excellent uplink</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113359417969990819",
			"parentId": "113359327416383425",
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff/113359417919985462",
			"author": {
				"name": "jake lazaroff",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/829/533/556/740/279/original/69a92e79689324bc.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-10-23T23:50:46.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mstdn.social/@peterreeves\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>peterreeves</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> MPAs can preload too (e.g. <a href=\"https://instant.page/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"\">instant.page/</span><span class=\"invisible\"></span></a>). i think optimizations like optimistic updates tend to make apps *significantly* more complex. imo the real opportunity for SPAs is adopting sync engines and/or a local-first architecture, where the network is not in the critical path of core functionality.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113359438389641695",
			"parentId": "113358904517967784",
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@a11yMel/113359438366603405",
			"author": {
